.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt PATINA-PROCESSES 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm patina-processes
.Nd the Patina desktop's process manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -processes | -performance
.Nm
.Fl h | V
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
shows what is running and what it costs the machine, and ends what has
hung.
The desktop opens it on
.Sy Ctrl+Shift+Escape ,
a key no config can take, which works over a fullscreen game too; pressed
again, it brings the open one forward.
.Pp
It has three views, as tabs in the title bar, and opens at the first:
.Bl -tag -width "Performance"
.It Overview
The machine's name, system, kernel and uptime; a tile for the CPU, memory,
each GPU, the disks and the network, with the last minute's graph, which a
click opens in Performance; and the ten busiest on the CPU, on the GPU and
on memory, apps counted whole.
Those lists hold still to be read: values are averaged over a few
readings, a row keeps its place until something clearly beats it, and the
list is sorted again only when a new one clearly leads.
A click shows one in Processes, a double click switches to an app, and a
right click has the menu below.
.It Processes
The apps first: each is the process whose windows are open, with every
process it started, such as a browser's content processes, counted
together; the arrow before its name lists them.
Then the background processes.
Each row shows the name, PID, user, CPU as a share of the whole machine,
memory in use, disk reading and writing and, where there is a GPU, its share
of the GPU, the numbers tinted by how much they are; a row's tooltip has the
GPU memory it holds.
A click on a column's name sorts by it, and again the other way.
The filter keeps what matches by name, command line, user or PID.
.It Performance
The CPU, memory, disks, each GPU and each network interface down the side,
each with a graph of the last minute; the CPU's and GPU's in their maker's
colour, NVIDIA green, AMD red and Intel blue.
The one chosen is drawn large, with each CPU's graph, the speed, load and
uptime, memory and swap, the space on each disk, the GPU's memory,
temperature, power, clocks, fan and video encoding and decoding, or the
interface's addresses, and the five things using it most; a click on one shows it in
Processes.
.El
.Pp
A right click on a row, in Processes or the Overview, or the Process menu,
offers:
.Bl -tag -width "Switch To"
.It Switch To
Bring the app's window forward.
.It End Task , End Process
Ask it to end
.Pq Dv SIGTERM ,
as closing it would.
An app's own process is asked; the rest follow it.
.It Kill
End it at once
.Pq Dv SIGKILL ,
for what does not listen; an app's processes all.
.It Pause , Resume
Stop it
.Pq Dv SIGSTOP
and let it go on
.Pq Dv SIGCONT .
.It Copy PID , Copy Command Line
.El
.Pp
Ending the compositor or the session it runs under is asked about first:
the desktop would go with it.
Processes of other users can be listed but not ended.
.Pp
View chooses how often the machine is read (twice a second to every four
seconds, or paused), whether to list only your processes, and the kernel's
threads, hidden by default.
.Sh KEYS
.Bl -tag -width "Shift+Delete"
.It Up , Down , Home , End
Choose a row.
.It Right , Left
Open or close an app's processes.
.It Enter
Switch to the app's window.
.It Delete
End it.
.It Shift+Delete
Kill it.
.It Ctrl+F
Filter; Escape clears it.
.It Alt+1 , Alt+2 , Alt+3
Overview, Processes and Performance; Ctrl+PageUp and Ctrl+PageDown step
along the tabs.
.It Ctrl+Q
Quit.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -processes
Open at the Processes view.
.It Fl -performance
Open at the Performance view.
.It Fl h , -help
Print a short usage message.
.It Fl V , -version
Print the version.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/patina-bar/config.toml
.Ic skin ,
the desktop's skin, which it wears and follows.
.It Pa /usr/local/share/applications/com.sectorflabs.ProcessManager.desktop
Its launcher entry, shown only on the Patina desktop.
.El
.Pp
GPUs are read as
.Xr nvtop 1
reads them: NVIDIA's through NVML, when its driver is installed, and the
others' through each process's
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fdinfo
and sysfs, on Linux.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 1 ,
.Xr patina-compositor 1 ,
.Xr patina-settings 1 ,
.Xr patina-desktop 7
